---
import FoundationsLayout from "../../components/FoundationsLayout.astro";
import SnippetBlock from "../../components/SnippetBlock.astro";
import { snippetFromLiteral, sourceSnippet } from "../../lib/snippet.ts";

const portDeclarations = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-declarations");
const connectFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-connect");
const graphFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-graph-helpers");
const effectiveFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-effective");
const localPlan = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-local-plan");
const replicatedPlan = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-replicated-plan");
const planFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-plan");

const flowDiagram = `typed component code             erased host boundary

Output(String) -- encode --> Json
      |                         |
      | output_descriptor       | PortRef + connection ID
      v                         v
Descriptor ---------> EffectiveGraph ---------> Delivery
                                                   |
                         target Input(String) <----+
                                decode + execute: component runtime`;
const flowDiagramSnippet = snippetFromLiteral(
  flowDiagram,
  "text",
  "component port flow",
);
---

<FoundationsLayout
  slug="ports"
  description="Watershed's port foundation uses payload types for direct connections, schema IDs for erased graph data, deterministic validation for an acyclic effective graph, and origin-aware dispatch planning."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Declare one direction of data flow</h2>
    <p>
      You send a typed payload out of a component through an output. Another
      component receives it through an input. Each declaration includes a
      stable port ID, a stable payload schema ID, and one side of the JSON
      codec. Gleam keeps the payload type attached while you work with the
      port.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={portDeclarations} />
    <p>
      Both <code>selected</code> and <code>focus_subject</code> use{" "}
      <code>String</code>, and both name the same{" "}
      <code>watershed/task-id@1</code> schema. The compiler rejects a direct
      connection to <code>Input(Int)</code>. <code>port.connect</code> then
      checks the schema IDs, catching two string-shaped payloads that mean
      different things.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={connectFn} />
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Local and collaborative describe the target effect</h2>
    <p>
      <code>LocalInput</code> changes presentation or controller state on the
      client where the event began: focus a subject, select a row, apply a
      filter. <code>CollaborativeInput</code> asks the target component on that
      same client to perform one of its own mutations. Watershed can then
      replicate the resulting channel operation in the normal way.
    </p>
    <p>
      The collaborative declaration lists capabilities such as{" "}
      <code>sequence:insert</code>. A host can show that shared-state effect
      before storing a connection. The current foundation records the list. A
      separate authorization layer must enforce policy.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>The origin client runs a collaborative input.</strong> It must
        submit the target mutation before it disconnects. The server cannot
        finish a job that no client submitted. Server-run retries would need a
        separate input class with execution records, idempotency, and
        authorization.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Erase payload types before storage</h2>
    <p>
      <code>output_descriptor</code> and <code>input_descriptor</code> remove
      the payload type but retain the port ID, direction, schema ID, and input
      class. A mixed component catalog lists this metadata, and a stored graph
      validates it. The typed input keeps the decoder a host uses when it
      executes a delivery.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={flowDiagramSnippet}>
      <figcaption slot="caption" class="annot">
        authors keep payload types; hosts store metadata
      </figcaption>
    </SnippetBlock>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Validate the stored graph before dispatch</h2>
    <p>
      A stored connection consists of two <code>PortRef</code> values and an
      edge ID. It can name a missing instance, a retired port, two outputs, or
      schemas that no longer match. Two clients can also add edges that look
      safe alone but form a cycle after their connection sets merge.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>port_graph.effective</code> derives the graph a client may use. It
      sorts edges by ID, rejects every duplicate ID, validates both endpoints,
      and accepts edges in that stable order until the next edge would create
      a cycle. Clients that read the same stored set derive the same acyclic
      result.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={effectiveFn} />
    <p>
      Rejected edges remain in storage. The effective graph returns diagnostics
      for duplicates, missing instances or ports, wrong directions, schema
      mismatches, and cycles. A UI can explain the dropped connection without
      destroying data another client may still understand.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={graphFn}>
      <figcaption slot="caption" class="annot">
        stored instance and port IDs describe two typed relationships
      </figcaption>
    </SnippetBlock>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Dispatch local intent once</h2>
    <p>
      A user action can emit a component output. Suppress that output while
      applying the replicated channel change, or each observer repeats the
      target mutation. <code>dispatch.plan</code> makes the origin explicit
      and returns no deliveries for{" "}
      <code>ReplicatedChange</code>.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={localPlan} />
    <SnippetBlock snippet={replicatedPlan} />
    <p>
      A local plan follows the effective graph's stable edge order. Each
      <code>Delivery</code> records the trace, edge ID, target port, target
      input class, and encoded JSON payload. The trace lets a host join later
      diagnostics to the event that started them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Planning checks the catalog again</h2>
    <p>
      A graph can outlive the descriptor snapshot that validated it. Before it
      creates deliveries, <code>dispatch.plan</code> re-reads the source and
      each target. A missing or redirected port becomes{" "}
      <code>SourceUnavailable</code> or <code>TargetUnavailable</code>; a
      changed schema becomes <code>SchemaChanged</code>. Valid sibling edges
      still produce deliveries.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={planFn} />
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>The browser runtime follows the whole trace</h3>
    <p>
      Watershed's JavaScript component runtime takes the plan from data to
      execution. It decodes target payloads, calls typed component handlers,
      stores their next running values, queues cascading outputs, and remembers
      which edges the trace already crossed. A local input changes only the
      origin's controller state. A collaborative input submits once on the
      origin; ordinary channel replication carries the mutation to peers
      without sending it through the graph again.{" "}
      <a href="/foundations/workspaces">The workspace layer</a> gives the
      runtime the persisted manifest and graph it keeps reconciled.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">The route</span>
    <p>
      Gleam checks payload types for direct connections. After type erasure,
      the host checks schema IDs and descriptors. The effective graph turns an
      invalid stored edge set into one deterministic acyclic view. Dispatch
      plans ordered deliveries for local intent and suppresses a replicated
      echo. Next:{" "}
      <a href="/foundations/workspaces">how a workspace keeps instances, order,
      and wiring in one document without throwing unfamiliar data away</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</FoundationsLayout>
